# Line endings are decided here, by the repository, and not by whatever
# core.autocrlf happens to be set to on each contributor's machine.
#
# Without this file a file committed with CRLF and a clone with autocrlf=true
# disagree permanently: git normalises CRLF to LF on the way into the index,
# so the working copy never matches and `git status` reports the file as
# modified forever. That is not cosmetic. A permanently dirty tree trains
# people to ignore `git status`, and it makes `git checkout` refuse to switch
# branches over a file nobody edited.

* text=auto

# Windows batch files. cmd.exe requires CRLF, so these are checked out with
# CRLF on every platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts stay LF even when checked out on Windows. A CRLF in the
# shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "sh\r", and the
# error it prints does not say so. CI runs ./gradlew on Linux.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Vitest snapshots. Written by the test runner, which always writes LF, so LF
# is what they are checked out as on every platform. Left to `text=auto` they
# arrive as CRLF on a Windows clone, the first `pnpm test` rewrites them to LF,
# and the file is reported as modified with no content change from then on —
# #211's failure again, in a file that only goes wrong after a test run rather
# than after a clone.
#
# It matters more here than the dirty tree suggests. A snapshot that is always
# modified is a snapshot a reviewer learns to skip, and CLAUDE.md is explicit
# that accepting one without reading what changed is the same failure as
# deleting the test.
*.snap text eol=lf

# Binary, never normalised and never diffed as text.
*.jar binary
*.patch text eol=lf
